  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a simple mechanical model for how a membrane-bound polymer can bend a membrane. On the left, a flexible polymer is attached near a flat membrane. A note indicates that attachment reduces the polymer’s radius of gyration, meaning the chain becomes more compact. This compaction creates an effective downward or outward pushing force on the membrane, shown by red arrows where the polymer contacts the surface.\n\nThe image then transitions to the right, where the same pushing is associated with visible membrane deformation. Instead of remaining flat, the membrane curves upward into a small peak beneath the polymer attachment site. The polymer still occupies space above the membrane, and the red arrows again emphasize localized pressure at the contact points. The central idea is that polymer confinement or crowding after attachment generates steric pressure, and that pressure is sufficient to produce membrane curvature.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys a cause-and-effect relationship: polymer attachment changes polymer size and shape, this creates mechanical pushing on the membrane, and that pushing leads to membrane bending. The schematic is qualitative rather than quantitative, but the mechanism is visually clear.",
